Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Firm indurated erythematous nodule, Thick adherent keratotic scale, Rapid localized growth

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-039
Reason for Visit: Acute, spontaneous, heavy bleeding from a rapidly growing, painful nodule on the hand, unmanageable with basic first aid.
Location: Nursing Home
Present at Visit: Patient, Registered Nurse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an elderly resident with a rapidly enlarging, painful nodule on the dorsal hand that had suddenly begun bleeding heavily and spontaneously. The nursing staff were unable to achieve hemostasis with standard pressure. Urgent assessment was required to safely arrest the hemorrhage, evaluate the lesion for high-risk malignant features, and initiate an expedited surgical referral pathway before the next routine GP visit.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "This hard lump on my hand has been growing for weeks and throbbing, and today it just burst open and won't stop bleeding."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the presence of a firm indurated erythematous nodule on the heavily sun-exposed dorsal aspect of the left hand. Over the past 6 weeks, there has been a rapid localized growth of the lesion, which was previously a flat, pre-existing actinic keratosis. The nodule developed a thick adherent keratotic scale (cutaneous horn). Over the past 24 hours, the patient noted increasing tenderness and pain upon palpation. Two hours ago, the thick scale spontaneously detached, revealing a central ulceration with a necrotic base that began bleeding profusely. The rapid growth and acute hemorrhage prompted the urgent call by facility staff.
Functional Status: Nursing home resident, utilizes a walking frame.
Social/Safety Concerns: Frailty; risk of significant blood loss due to anticoagulant use and uncontrolled bleeding.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 152/90 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, mildly anxious, sitting with the left hand elevated and wrapped in blood-soaked gauze.
Physical Exam: On the dorsal surface of the left hand, there is a 2.5 cm firm, deeply indurated erythematous nodule. The center of the lesion demonstrates a deep ulceration with a necrotic, crusted base that is actively oozing venous blood. A large fragment of thick, keratotic scale is partially attached to the periphery. The nodule is highly tender to palpation and feels tethered to the underlying subcutaneous tissue, lacking free mobility. There is no surrounding cellulitic erythema or purulence. Palpation of the left axillary and epitrochlear lymph nodes reveals a firm, 1.5 cm mobile node in the axilla.
Environmental Observations: Standard nursing facility room.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient is on Apixaban 5mg BD for atrial fibrillation, significantly exacerbating the acute hemorrhage.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) with acute ulceration and hemorrhage - Status: Acute, high priority
Differentials:

Keratoacanthoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: While clinically similar with rapid growth, the lesion lacks the classic symmetrical, dome-shaped architecture with a central keratin plug, presenting instead as a deeply indurated, ulcerated, asymmetrical mass highly suspicious for invasive SCC.

Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The rapid growth over weeks, thick keratotic scale, and deep induration, which heavily favor SCC over the typically slow-growing, pearly, telangiectatic presentation of a BCC.

Infected Actinic Keratosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound deep induration, nodular growth, and lack of response to simple topical therapies; it has clearly evolved past a superficial precancerous lesion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (due to anticoagulant use, bleeding risk, and malignant potential)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an actively bleeding, rapidly growing, deeply indurated nodule on a sun-damaged hand, exhibiting the classic macroscopic features of an invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma. Triage correctly prioritized the uncontrolled bleeding in an elderly patient on Apixaban as a high-risk scenario requiring urgent out-of-hours intervention. The immediate clinical necessity was to achieve definitive mechanical and chemical hemostasis that nursing staff were unable to accomplish. Furthermore, the presence of deep induration and a palpable axillary node mandated immediate, explicit communication with the primary GP to initiate a fast-track surgical oncology referral, preventing potentially fatal delays in managing an aggressive, bleeding malignancy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Achieved hemostasis utilizing firm direct pressure, application of silver nitrate to the bleeding ulcer base, and application of a thick alginate (hemostatic) dressing. Secured with a firm crepe bandage for sustained pressure. Instructed RNs to elevate the limb overnight.
Positive Behavioral Change: By keeping your hand elevated on a pillow tonight, you will use gravity to lower the blood pressure in your hand, ensuring the clot we just formed remains strong and the bleeding does not restart. By leaving this thick bandage completely untouched for the next 3 days, you will protect the fragile ulcer and prevent another frightening bleeding episode while we organize the surgeon.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Initiated urgent fast-track referral to a specialist General Surgeon/Plastics for excision and nodal evaluation.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Handover provided to the facility RNs. Urgent primary GP review required within 48 hours to manage the surgical referral pathway.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the bandage becomes completely saturated with fresh blood that drips continuously despite 20 minutes of firm, direct pressure and limb elevation, nursing staff must arrange immediate transfer to the Emergency Department for surgical hemostasis.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This lesion is highly suspicious for an aggressive skin cancer. Definitive diagnosis and treatment require surgical removal and laboratory analysis.
Clinical Course: The bleeding has been mechanically stopped. The lump will continue to be painful and will not heal without surgical excision.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not bump or use the left hand forcefully. Apply continuous pressure and notify nursing staff immediately if you feel warm fluid (blood) leaking under the bandage.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The rapid growth of this lump has created very fragile blood vessels that burst today, made worse by your blood-thinning medication. The chemical stick and tight bandage have sealed the leak. The lump itself needs to be cut out by a surgeon very soon.
Actionable Steps: Keep the hand strictly elevated when sitting or sleeping. Do not get the bandage wet.

